  "worked_solution": "We first examine the numerators and denominators of the corresponding sequence.\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2},\\,\\frac{5}{4},\\,\\frac{5}{6},\\,\u2026,\\,\\frac{5}{26},\\,\\frac{5}{28}\n$$\n\nOur goal is to find a pattern in the terms. To do this, we note the following:\n\n- The numerators form the following sequence:\n\n$$\n5,\\,5,\\,5,\\,\u2026,\\,5,\\,5\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of this sequence is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=5.\n$$\n\n- The denominators form the following sequence:\n\n$$\n2,\\,4,\\,\u2026,\\,26,\\,28\n$$\n\nThese values are multiples of $2,$ and since $28\u00f72=14,$ there are $14$ terms in total. The $n$th term of this sequence is given by\n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=2n,\\,1\\le n\\le 14.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $n$th term of the sequence\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{2},\\,\\frac{5}{4},\\,\\frac{5}{6},\\,\u2026,\\,\\frac{5}{26},\\,\\frac{5}{28}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{b}_{n}}=\\frac{5}{2n},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ ranges from $n=1$ to $n=14.$ \n\nTherefore, we can express our series using sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S={\\sum }_{n=1}^{14}\\frac{5}{2n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
